{# Resolve one list by its server-relative URL rather than by its title.
   Expects `apiUrl` and `odataName`, both emitted by `_site_guard.js.j2`.

   WHY NOT getbytitle. A list renamed in place keeps the slug it was created
   with, which is this project's own documented behaviour and the point of
   `renamed_from`. So on any site that has been through a rename, the segment
   the address bar shows is NOT the list's title, and every script that
   resolves by that segment 404s on its first read. Seen live 2026-09-03
   (issue #385): lists answering at /Lists/ProgramRisk/ titled GOV_Risk.

   `web/GetList` takes the server-relative URL instead, which is the string
   the operator actually copied. Microsoft Learn, "Working with lists and
   list items with REST", documents the alias-parameter form used here.

   The deploy, rollback, verify and assess scripts keep getbytitle and are
   right to: the mapping DECLARES those titles and the deploy renames lists
   to match. Only the operator-pasted scripts infer a name from a URL. #}
  // The alias value is ONE OData string literal: quote it, double any
  // embedded apostrophe, then percent-encode the whole literal including its
  // quotes, which is the form Learn's example shows.
  //
  // NOT `odataName`, deliberately. That helper doubles the apostrophe AND
  // calls encodeURIComponent, which is right where the literal is already
  // inside a path segment; here the quotes have to be inside the encoding
  // too, so reusing it would encode the path twice and every read would 404
  // on a percent sign. Doubling is inlined for that reason, not by oversight.
  //
  // The quotes are escaped too. encodeURIComponent leaves `'` alone, and
  // Learn's example carries `%27` delimiters, so they are encoded here rather
  // than left raw on the assumption SharePoint accepts a bare one.
  const odataPathLiteral = (path) =>
    encodeURIComponent(`'${String(path).replace(/'/g, "''")}'`).replace(/'/g, '%27');
  const listByPathUrl = (path, query = '') =>
    apiUrl(`web/GetList(@listUrl)?@listUrl=${odataPathLiteral(path)}${query}`);
